@page "/components/sortable"
@page "/components/sortable-list"

<PageOutlet Url="components/sortable"
            Title="Sortable"
            Description="BitSortable is a drag-and-drop sortable list: reorder its items, or drag them between lists of a group, by pointer, touch or keyboard." />

<DemoPage Name="Sortable"
          SecondaryNames="@(["SortableList", "ReorderableList", "DragAndDropList"])"
          Description="BitSortable is a drag-and-drop sortable list, inspired by SortableJS: reorder the items of a list, or drag them between lists that share a Group - with live reordering, slide animations, clone and move pull modes, swap thresholds, drag handles, filters, nested lists, swap mode, multi-drag and the keyboard, every step announced to screen readers."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubExtrasUrl="Sortable/BitSortable.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Extras/Sortable/BitSortableDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.Extras"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The list needs no service of its own: lists exchange items through a coordinator they share per scope - one per
            circuit on Blazor Server, one per app on WebAssembly - so two users never share a drag. It changes the page's own
            IList in place and raises ItemsChanged once a drag changed it, so it is bound with @@bind-Items to a field
            declared as an IList&lt;TItem&gt; (a List&lt;TItem&gt;, or any list that is not read-only); a field of another
            type does not compile with @@bind-Items. Its items are told apart the way @@key tells them apart - by their
            Equals, or by the key ItemKey gives them - so a list must not hold the same item twice. The look of the list
            and its items is the page's: the component's stylesheet only draws the drag states.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The sorting engine is C#, not SortableJS behind JavaScript interop: which list takes an item, what a position
            means, every change to the bound lists and every event are decided in .NET, and Blazor renders the result -
            nothing moves the DOM behind its back, so there is nothing to reconcile. The script of the component does what
            has to happen where the pointer is: it follows the drag, measures the pointer against the items, plays the slide
            animations, and calls .NET only when the drop position changes, so a drag stays smooth on Blazor Server too.
            Every item is wrapped in an element of the list, rendered with @@key, that carries the drag; ItemTemplate
            renders inside it. When an item moves, Blazor moves its existing element, which keeps the browser's drag alive
            while the list reorders under it.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            A list drags only on a page that renders interactively - WebAssembly, Server, Auto or Hybrid. A statically
            rendered page shows the items but never drags them; a prerendered one shows them at once and starts dragging as
            soon as it is interactive.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            When something does not work: items that render but never drag are on a page that is not interactive. An item
            that another list does not take needs both lists in the same Group (or the target's PutGroups naming the
            source's group) and of the same TItem, a source whose Pull is not None and that is not at its MinItems, and a
            target with Put that is neither Disabled, full (MaxItems) nor read-only and does not already hold an equal item
            (or one with the same ItemKey). Drag states that are not drawn - the dragged item not dimmed, the original not
            hidden while it is previewed in another list - mean the Extras stylesheet is not linked.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Bind the list with <b>@@bind-Items</b> and render each item with <b>ItemTemplate</b>. Drag an item: the list
                reorders live as the pointer moves, the other items slide out of its way, and the bound list is changed in place,
                so the order a user makes is the order the page reads back. <b>ItemsChanged</b> is raised once per drag, after
                the drop, and only when the order actually changed: a drag that ends where it started raises nothing.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Drag Item 1 down past Item 3: it moves as soon as the pointer enters the next item, and the order below changes when it is dropped.</li>
                <li>Without a mouse: Tab into the list, pick the focused item up with Space, move it with the arrow keys, and drop it with Space - or put it back with Escape (see Keyboard &amp; Strings).</li>
                <li>On a touch screen, rest a finger on an item for a moment to pick it up; a quick swipe still scrolls the page (see Touch &amp; pen).</li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="basicItems">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                            <div class="item">@item</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Order: @string.Join(", ", basicItems)</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Animation" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                <b>Animation</b> is how long, in milliseconds, the items take to slide into their new places (150 by default);
                0 turns the slide off. Before each change the list records where every item is, lets Blazor update the page,
                then slides each item from its old place to its new one (the FLIP technique). A move that starts while a slide
                is still running picks it up from where the item is, so nothing jumps. Like every animation of the library it
                follows the reduced-motion preference: with the system's setting on, nothing slides. Move the slider to 1000 to
                watch the slide in slow motion.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitSlider Label="Animation (ms)" Min="0" Max="1000" Step="50" @bind-Value="animation" />
                </div>
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="animationItems" Animation="(int)animation">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                            <div class="item">@item</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Group" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                Lists with the same <b>Group</b> exchange items: drag an item from one list onto an item of the other, or into its
                empty space. While it is over the other list a placeholder shows where it will land. <b>ItemClass</b> gives an
                item's wrapper a class chosen per item, which follows the item from list to list - the B items keep their mark
                wherever they go, and so does the placeholder.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Over another list the item is not moved yet: that list renders a placeholder - the item's own template, with
                    the ghost look - where it would land, and the source hides its original. The item moves between the lists
                    when the drag ends, since moving an element from one component to another would recreate it and cancel the
                    browser's drag. A keyboard drag moves the item itself.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Drag an item over the other list, then back over its own: the preview goes away and the item is home again.
                    Released with the mouse outside every list, an item previewed in another one lands there, as in SortableJS;
                    RevertOnSpill puts it back instead (see CanDrop, ConfirmDrop &amp; RevertOnSpill).
                </li>
                <li>
                    An item is only offered to lists of its own TItem, and never to a list that already holds an equal item - two
                    lists of strings cannot both hold "A1". Lists of other item types may share the group: they ignore each
                    other's items.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Each list that a drop changed raises its own ItemsChanged once. Give the lists an <b>Id</b> when the page
                    needs to tell them apart: the events report it as FromId and ToId.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">List A</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="groupA" Group="shared" ItemClass="@(item => item.StartsWith('B') ? "tinted" : null)">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">List B</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="groupB" Group="shared" ItemClass="@(item => item.StartsWith('B') ? "tinted" : null)">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="order">A: @string.Join(", ", groupA)</div>
                <div class="order">B: @string.Join(", ", groupB)</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="EmptyText & EmptyTemplate" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                A list with no items says so, and still takes a drop. <b>EmptyTemplate</b> is what it shows then, <b>EmptyText</b>
                a text in its place, and with neither it shows the NoItemsFound text of BitStrings. What an empty list shows is
                the one entry of the list while it has no items, which keeps an empty list tall enough to drop an item into and is
                read as part of it; it gives way to the placeholder while the list previews an item. ChildContent, rendered after
                the items whether there are any or not, is not the place for an empty state. Move every message out of the
                Inbox, then drag them back.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Inbox</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="inbox" Group="mail" EmptyText="Inbox zero - nothing left to sort.">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Archive</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="mailArchive" Group="mail">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                            <EmptyTemplate>
                                <div class="empty">
                                    <BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.Archive" />
                                    Nothing archived yet - drop a message here.
                                </div>
                            </EmptyTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Spam</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="spam" Group="mail">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Clone" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                With <b>Pull</b> set to <b>Clone</b>, what leaves a list is a copy and the original stays, so the same item can be
                dragged out again and again; with the mouse, the cursor shows a copy while the item is over another list. The
                <b>Clone</b> factory makes the copy; without one an ICloneable item is cloned and any other is shared. The palette
                here is a plain array: a read-only list - an array, a ReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;, an ImmutableList&lt;T&gt; - is
                shown and cloned from, but never sorted, pulled from or dropped into, so it needs no Sort or Put of its own and is
                bound one way, with <b>Items</b>. A clone source that is a mutable list still sorts within itself and takes items
                back: only what leaves it is a copy, and what is dragged out of the form moves as usual.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The copy must be a distinct item: a list holds an item once, so a copy that equals an item already in the
                    target is refused. That is why the fields here are a class, compared by reference - two records with the
                    same values would be equal, and the second one refused - and why a shared item works for an immutable
                    reference type but not for the same string twice. With ItemKey, the factory gives a copy a key of its own.
                </li>
                <li>
                    In the events that follow the drop, Item is the copy and IsClone is true, and OnRemove is not raised: nothing
                    left the source.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Combined with Swap, a clone source gives its item itself away in the swap rather than a copy; combined with
                    MultiDrag, dragging into another list clones only the dragged item.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Palette</div>
                        <BitSortable Items="palette" Group="form" Pull="BitSortablePull.Clone" Clone="@(field => new FormField(field.Name))">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="field">
                                <div class="item">@field.Name</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Form</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="form" Group="form" EmptyText="Drag fields from the palette.">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="field">
                                <div class="item">@field.Name</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Form: @string.Join(", ", form.Select(f => f.Name))</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Sort, Put & Pull" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                Each list says what it allows. <b>Sort</b> set to false keeps its own order, <b>Put</b> set to false refuses the
                items of other lists, and <b>Pull</b> set to <b>None</b> keeps its items in it while it still sorts and takes
                items in. The items of the first list can only be dragged out; those of the second never leave.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Sort="false": no reordering within, items still drag out, and items of other lists still drop in where they are dropped - Sort keeps the list's own items in their order, it does not refuse others.</li>
                <li>Put="false": reorders within and drags out, but takes nothing in.</li>
                <li>Pull="BitSortablePull.None": reorders within and takes items in, but nothing drags out.</li>
                <li>Pull="BitSortablePull.Clone": reorders within and takes items in, and what drags out is a copy (see Clone).</li>
                <li>Disabled: none of these (see Disabled).</li>
                <li>A read-only list, such as an array, behaves as if it had Sort="false", Put="false" and a Pull of None - except that a Clone pull still copies from it.</li>
                <li>Try it: drag Pick 1 down its list - nothing moves; drag it into Target - it goes; drag it back - the first list does not take it. Drag Gym into Pinned: it goes in, and now it stays there.</li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Pick from (no Sort, no Put)</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="pickFrom" Group="rules" Sort="false" Put="false">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Pinned (Pull None)</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="pinned" Group="rules" Pull="BitSortablePull.None">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Target</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="target" Group="rules">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                A <b>Disabled</b> list takes part in no drag: its items cannot be picked up - by the mouse, a finger or the
                keyboard - and nothing is dropped into it. Its items are no longer a tab stop, and its root carries the bit-dis
                class, the one hook for a disabled component. What its items render stays live. Use it to lock a list while,
                say, its contents are being saved; to lock single items instead, see Filter.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitToggle Label="Disabled" @bind-Value="archiveDisabled" />
                </div>
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Archive</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="archive" Group="desk" Disabled="archiveDisabled">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Desk</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="desk" Group="desk">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Handle" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <b>Handle</b> is the CSS selector of a drag handle inside each item. Only the handle starts a drag, and the rest of
                the item keeps its own pointer behavior - its text can be selected, its inputs typed into, its buttons clicked;
                here a checkbox that is checked with a click. A tick travels with its step, since it lives in the item.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    With a Handle the items render as not draggable; pressing an element that matches the selector makes its item
                    draggable for the drag that follows. The root gets the bit-srt-hnd class, which takes the grab cursor and the
                    no-select rule off the rest of the item, so give the handle a grab cursor of its own (this page's handle class
                    does).
                </li>
                <li>
                    From the keyboard nothing changes: Tab reaches the item (not the control inside it) and Space picks it up,
                    while keys pressed inside a control are the control's. For a handle a keyboard user reaches as well, make it
                    a button type="button" with an accessible name: it picks its item up too.
                </li>
                <li>
                    On a touch screen a handle still needs the long press, unless TouchDelay is 0 and the handle has
                    touch-action: none (see Touch &amp; pen).
                </li>
                <li>
                    A handle belongs to the item it sits in: in nested lists, a handle of an inner item never drags the outer item
                    around it. A selector that is not valid CSS breaks nothing: the script warns once in the console, and no item
                    drags.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="steps" Handle=".handle">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="step">
                            <div class="item row">
                                <BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.GripperDotsVertical" Class="handle" />
                                <BitCheckbox Label="@step.Text" @bind-Value="step.Done" />
                            </div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Steps: @string.Join(", ", steps.Select(s => s.Done ? $"{s.Text} (done)" : s.Text))</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Filter" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                <b>Filter</b> is asked about every item, and an item it returns true for cannot be picked up - by the mouse, a
                finger or the keyboard. It does not pin the item to its index: the other items can still be dragged past it, which
                shifts it along by one, as in SortableJS. Here it reads a flag of the task, which the button of the task toggles; a
                click on a control inside an item is the control's, and never starts a drag.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Filter is a predicate on the typed item, not a CSS selector as in SortableJS, so the rule lives next to the
                    data it reads, and a lock follows the data.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A locked item gets the not-allowed cursor and the Filtered part (Classes.Filtered, Styles.Filtered). It is not
                    dimmed by default, since its content stays live; --bit-Sortable-filtered-opacity fades it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    From the keyboard, Space on a locked item announces the Locked text of the strings ("Release notes cannot be
                    moved.").
                </li>
                <li>
                    It locks an item against the user's drags only: a move made by code (MoveAsync, MoveToAsync) still moves it,
                    and in a MultiDrag list a locked item cannot be selected. To lock a whole list, see Disabled.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="tasks" Filter="@(task => task.Locked)">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="task">
                            <div class="item row">
                                <span class="grow">@task.Title</span>
                                <BitButton IconName="@(task.Locked ? BitIconName.Lock : BitIconName.Unlock)"
                                           Variant="BitVariant.Text"
                                           Size="BitSize.Small"
                                           AriaLabel="@($"Lock {task.Title}")"
                                           OnClick="@(() => task.Locked = !task.Locked)" />
                            </div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Swap thresholds" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
            <div>
                <b>SwapThreshold</b> is the part of an item, across its middle, that the pointer has to reach before the dragged
                item swaps past it; a smaller one leaves a dead zone near each edge. <b>InvertSwap</b> moves the zones to the edges
                of the item, <b>InvertedSwapThreshold</b> wide, for a sort-between-items feel. <b>Direction</b> says along which
                axis the zones run. The tinted bands show the zones. This mirrors the SortableJS thresholds demo.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Set SwapThreshold to 1: the whole item is the zone, and the dragged item swaps as soon as the pointer enters
                    the next one. Set it to 0.2: the pointer has to reach the thin band in the middle, and the edges around it do
                    nothing.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Turn InvertSwap on: entering the top or bottom band of an item puts the dragged one before or after it - a
                    "drop between the items" feel. InvertedSwapThreshold sizes the two edge bands together; left at 0 it takes
                    SwapThreshold.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Swap an item past the next one and move the pointer back a little: the area the dragged item has just left -
                    its shadow - swaps it back. That rule, ported from SortableJS, keeps a short item and a tall one from trading
                    places back and forth while the pointer sits still.
                </li>
                <li>
                    An item that enters a list it is not in yet always uses the inverted zones, whatever InvertSwap says, so it can
                    be dropped before the first item as easily as after the last.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitSlider Label="SwapThreshold" Min="0.1" Max="1" Step="0.05" @bind-Value="swapThreshold" />
                    <BitToggle Label="InvertSwap" @bind-Value="invertSwap" />
                    <BitSlider Label="InvertedSwapThreshold" Min="0.1" Max="1" Step="0.05" @bind-Value="invertedSwapThreshold" />
                </div>
                <div class="panel zones @(invertSwap ? "inverted" : null)" style="@ZoneStyle">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="thresholdItems"
                                 Direction="BitSortableDirection.Vertical"
                                 SwapThreshold="swapThreshold"
                                 InvertSwap="invertSwap"
                                 InvertedSwapThreshold="invertedSwapThreshold">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                            <div class="item">@item</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Grid & horizontal lists" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example11CodeFiles" Id="example11">
            <div>
                The list does not mind its layout: laid out as a CSS grid it is a sortable grid, as a wrapping row a horizontal
                list. Its items are measured as they are laid out, so a grid sorts across a row and from row to row. The
                default <b>Direction</b>, Auto, tells the axis from the layout; the row of tags sets it.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Auto reads the first two items: side by side is horizontal, anything else vertical. With a single item in view
                    it reads the list instead: a flex row, or a grid of several columns, is horizontal, a flex column vertical.
                    Set Direction when the layout says otherwise - in a grid, whose first two items sit side by side although the
                    list also runs down.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The hit test is two-dimensional, so a grid sorts in every direction whatever Direction says: Direction only
                    decides which way an item's swap zones run (see Swap thresholds).
                </li>
                <li>
                    The item wrappers are the direct children of the list's root, so the grid or the flex row lays them out: style
                    them with ItemClass or Classes.Item, or style the template inside them. A list reversed with flex-direction:
                    row-reverse or column-reverse is measured in its own order, as a right-to-left one is.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Drag square 1 onto square 7, a row down: it sorts across rows, not only along one. From the keyboard the
                    arrows follow the layout: left and right along a row, up and down from row to row.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panel grid">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="numbers">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="number">
                            <div class="tile">@number</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="panel strip">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="tags" Direction="BitSortableDirection.Horizontal">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="tag">
                            <div class="chip">@tag</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Nested lists" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
            <div>
                A list can render another one inside each of its items. Every level shares the group, so an item - with its
                children - moves from branch to branch and from depth to depth. Each item renders a list of its own, empty or not,
                so any item can take children. Each list is bound one way, with Items, to its item's children, which it changes in
                place; ItemsChanged only tells the page to render the tree again. SwapThreshold 0.65 is the value the SortableJS
                nested demo uses: the outer part of each item is a dead zone.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    An item is never dropped inside itself: the lists in the dragged item's own subtree, and those inside the drop
                    preview, refuse it - drag Item 1.1 over its own children and none takes it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Each list handles its own items only: a drag that starts in an inner list is not also a drag of the outer item
                    around it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A nested list takes the pointer only for a drag it could receive. One of another group, of another item type,
                    or with Put="false" leaves the drag to the list around it - which is how the columns of a board still sort
                    while the pointer crosses the card lists inside them.
                </li>
                <li>From the keyboard, the arrows carry a picked-up item into a nested list and out again, in the direction they point.</li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panel tree">
                    @NestedList(tree)
                </div>
                <div class="order">Tree: @Describe(tree)</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="PutGroups" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
            <div>
                <b>PutGroups</b> names the groups whose items a list takes besides its own. Tasks and Bugs are groups of their own
                and never exchange items, while the Trash takes both. It lets items in only: what is in the Trash stays there.
                Put, MaxItems, CanDrop and the item type apply to those items as to any other drop, and the events' FromGroup
                says which group an item came from.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Tasks</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="todo" Group="tasks">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Bugs</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="bugs" Group="bugs">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Trash</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="trash" Group="trash" PutGroups="@(["tasks", "bugs"])" EmptyText="Drop a task or a bug here.">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="MaxItems & MinItems" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
            <div>
                <b>MaxItems</b> is the most items a list holds: once full, it takes nothing from other lists - the pointer passes
                over it to the list around it, or shows that it cannot drop. <b>MinItems</b> is the fewest it keeps: nothing is
                moved out of it that would leave fewer, and the other lists do not take the drag, as if they were full. Both lists
                still sort their own items; a list at its minimum still clones out and swaps, none of which empties it. A
                multi-drag counts every item it carries, and a swap brings one item and takes one, so it is never refused for room.
                Drag Deploy to the Shelf - it goes, and two steps are left; Test then stays in the pipeline until a step comes back.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Pipeline (at least 2)</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="pipeline" Group="limits" MinItems="2">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Shelf (at most 3)</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="shelf" Group="limits" MaxItems="3">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="CanDrop, ConfirmDrop & RevertOnSpill" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
            <div>
                <b>CanDrop</b> is asked, as the dragged item reaches a position, whether it may take it - here Done takes cards from
                In progress only (<b>FromId</b> is the <b>Id</b> of the list the item comes from). <b>ConfirmDrop</b> is asked once
                the item is dropped, for a check that has to wait: refused, every list goes back as it was. With
                <b>RevertOnSpill</b>, a card released outside every list that takes it goes back where it came from.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    CanDrop is the SortableJS onMove: it is asked for a reorder within the list and for an item coming from
                    another list alike, before anything is shown, with the proposed move - Item (and Items for a multi-drag),
                    FromId and FromGroup, OldIndex, and NewIndex, the index the item would have here. Refused, the item stays
                    where it was, nothing is previewed, and the pointer shows that it cannot drop. It runs while the pointer
                    moves: keep it fast and free of side effects.
                </li>
                <li>
                    ConfirmDrop lets a server or a dialog have the last word. The drop shows at once and the lists stay as dropped
                    while it runs, and no other drag starts until it answers. True keeps the move, and the usual events follow.
                    False - or a task that throws - slides every list the drag changed back, raises ItemsChanged for them, and
                    OnEnd reports Canceled, with no OnUpdate, OnAdd, OnRemove or OnSort.
                </li>
                <li>
                    RevertOnSpill goes on the list the drag starts in. Without it a mouse drag released outside every list keeps
                    what it did, as in SortableJS. A keyboard, touch or pen drag cancelled with Escape is always put back.
                </li>
                <li>
                    While a card is dragged, the columns that take it are outlined, and a column that refuses the position turns
                    red: the script marks the lists with attributes, which cost no render, and the --bit-Sortable-accepts-outline,
                    -over-outline and -refused-outline variables set on the board draw them (see Style &amp; Class).
                </li>
                <li>
                    Turn the server's approval off and drag a card into Done: it shows there while the server thinks, then slides
                    back. From the keyboard the same rules apply: carry a card toward Done and the refusal is announced ("Plan the
                    launch cannot be moved there."), and the card stays put.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitToggle Label="The server approves drops into Done" @bind-Value="approve" />
                </div>
                <div class="panels" style="@boardOutlines">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">To do</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="boardTodo" Group="board" Id="board-todo" RevertOnSpill>
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">In progress</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="boardDoing" Group="board" Id="board-doing" RevertOnSpill>
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Done (from In progress)</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="boardDone"
                                     Group="board"
                                     Id="board-done"
                                     RevertOnSpill
                                     CanDrop="CanDropInDone"
                                     ConfirmDrop="ConfirmDoneAsync">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Server: @serverStatus</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="MoveAsync & MoveToAsync" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example16CsharpCode" Id="example16">
            <div>
                Dragging is not the only way to move an item. <b>MoveAsync</b> moves one within its list and <b>MoveToAsync</b> into
                another list, named by its <b>Id</b>, as a drag would - the rules apply, ConfirmDrop is asked, the items slide, the
                events follow with Input set to Api, and the move is announced when it lands. They are the move-up and move-to
                buttons and item menus that WCAG 2.5.7 (dragging movements) asks an accessible app to offer beside dragging, and
                that some screen readers in browse mode need. Both return true when the item moved, and refuse while a drag is in
                progress. A Filter does not stop them, and a multi-drag selection is not carried along.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Backlog</div>
                        <BitSortable @ref="backlogList" @bind-Items="backlog" Group="sprint" Id="move-backlog">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item row">
                                    <span class="grow">@item</span>
                                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Up"
                                               Variant="BitVariant.Text"
                                               Size="BitSize.Small"
                                               AriaLabel="@($"Move {item} up")"
                                               OnClick="@(() => backlogList.MoveAsync(item, backlog.IndexOf(item) - 1))" />
                                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Down"
                                               Variant="BitVariant.Text"
                                               Size="BitSize.Small"
                                               AriaLabel="@($"Move {item} down")"
                                               OnClick="@(() => backlogList.MoveAsync(item, backlog.IndexOf(item) + 1))" />
                                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Forward"
                                               Variant="BitVariant.Text"
                                               Size="BitSize.Small"
                                               AriaLabel="@($"Move {item} to the sprint")"
                                               OnClick="@(() => backlogList.MoveToAsync(item, "move-sprint", sprint.Count))" />
                                </div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Sprint</div>
                        <BitSortable @ref="sprintList" @bind-Items="sprint" Group="sprint" Id="move-sprint">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item row">
                                    <span class="grow">@item</span>
                                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Back"
                                               Variant="BitVariant.Text"
                                               Size="BitSize.Small"
                                               AriaLabel="@($"Move {item} back to the backlog")"
                                               OnClick="@(() => sprintList.MoveToAsync(item, "move-backlog", backlog.Count))" />
                                </div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Swap" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example17CsharpCode" Id="example17">
            <div>
                In <b>Swap</b> mode the dragged item trades places with the item it is dropped on, and nothing moves meanwhile:
                the item under the pointer is highlighted instead (the SwapTarget part), and an item dragged back over itself
                highlights nothing and changes nothing. The events of a swap say so with <b>IsSwap</b>, and <b>SwappedWith</b>
                names the other item, so that a handler can save both moves. This mirrors the SortableJS Swap plugin.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Between lists, an item dropped onto an item of the other list trades places with it, and each list keeps its
                    length. Each list must be able to take the other's item: the same group, a Pull that lets the item go, and a
                    Put that lets it in. A full list (MaxItems) never refuses a swap for room, and a Clone source gives its item
                    itself away in a swap rather than a copy.
                </li>
                <li>
                    From the keyboard, the arrows of a picked-up item move the highlight instead of the item, announced as
                    "Item 1 will trade places with Item 3.", and Space makes the swap.
                </li>
                <li>The highlight is drawn with --bit-Sortable-swap-background and --bit-Sortable-swap-outline-color; Classes.SwapTarget gives it a look of its own.</li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="swapItems" TItem="string" Swap OnUpdate="HandleSwap">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                            <div class="item">@item</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Order: @string.Join(", ", swapItems)</div>
                <div class="order">Last swap: @lastSwap</div>
                <br />
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Left</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="swapLeft" Group="swap" Swap>
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Right</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="swapRight" Group="swap" Swap>
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="MultiDrag" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example18CsharpCode" Id="example18">
            <div>
                With <b>MultiDrag</b>, a click selects an item and another click deselects it, and Shift+click selects a range;
                dragging any selected item carries the whole selection, which keeps its order and lands as one block where it is
                dropped. <b>@@bind-SelectedItems</b> keeps the selection and a field of the page in step, both ways. Both lists of
                the second pair share a group, so a selection made in one is dragged into the other. This mirrors the SortableJS
                multi-drag demo.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The selection is spent by the move, and OnEnd's Items lists every item the drag carried. Dragging an item that
                    is not selected clears the selection, and only that item moves.
                </li>
                <li>
                    From the keyboard, Shift+Space selects or deselects the focused item, announced as "Item 2 selected. 1
                    item selected."; Space on a selected item picks the whole selection up, and the arrows move it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    SelectedItemsChanged reports the selection, in list order, whenever it changes - a click, a range, Shift+Space,
                    a drag that spends it or one that clears it. A selection the page sets leaves out the items that are not in the
                    list and those a Filter locks; items that leave the list, or are replaced with the whole Items list, leave the
                    selection too.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The selected items wear the Selected part, drawn with --bit-Sortable-selected-background and
                    --bit-Sortable-selected-ring-color, and the picture a multi-drag carries shows how many items it moves
                    (--bit-Sortable-count-background, --bit-Sortable-count-color).
                </li>
                <li>
                    With a Clone pull, dragging into another list clones only the dragged item. A move made by code (MoveAsync)
                    moves one item and does not carry the selection along.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => selectedItems = multiItems.ToList())">Select all</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => selectedItems = [])">Clear the selection</BitButton>
                </div>
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="multiItems" @bind-SelectedItems="selectedItems" MultiDrag>
                        <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                            <div class="item">@item</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Selected: @(selectedItems.Count == 0 ? "nothing" : string.Join(", ", selectedItems))</div>
                <br />
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Left</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="multiLeft" Group="multi" MultiDrag>
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Right</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="multiRight" Group="multi" MultiDrag>
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode" Id="example19">
            <div>
                A drag raises <b>OnStart</b> on the list it starts in, <b>OnChange</b> each time the item takes a new position,
                <b>OnUpdate</b> when a list's own order changed, <b>OnRemove</b> and <b>OnAdd</b> when an item crossed from one list
                into another, <b>OnSort</b> on every list it changed, and <b>OnEnd</b> on the source list, whatever happened.
                Each comes with a <b>BitSortableEventArgs</b>. The newest event is on top of the log.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The completion events are raised after the bound lists have changed and ItemsChanged has been raised, so a
                    handler sees the final state. Within a list: OnUpdate, OnSort, OnEnd. Across lists: OnRemove on the source
                    (not for a clone), OnAdd on the destination, OnSort on the source (not for a clone) and on the destination,
                    then OnEnd. A drag that changed nothing, or was cancelled, raises OnEnd alone, with Canceled set when it was
                    put back.
                </li>
                <li>
                    They are all raised once the drag is over, so a handler that takes its time - a save to a server, say - never
                    holds up the next drag.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Try it: drag Item 1 below Item 3 (OnStart, then OnUpdate, OnSort and OnEnd); drag Item 4 into the right list
                    (OnRemove on the left, OnAdd on the right, OnSort on both, OnEnd on the left); drag an item into the other list
                    and back home before dropping it (OnStart and OnEnd only); pick an item up with Space, move it and press Escape
                    (OnEnd alone, keyboard and canceled).
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitToggle Label="Log OnChange too" @bind-Value="logChange" />
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => eventLog.Clear())">Clear the log</BitButton>
                </div>
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">events-left</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="eventsLeft"
                                     TItem="string"
                                     Group="events"
                                     Id="events-left"
                                     OnStart="@(e => Log("OnStart", "events-left", e))"
                                     OnChange="@(e => Log("OnChange", "events-left", e))"
                                     OnUpdate="@(e => Log("OnUpdate", "events-left", e))"
                                     OnRemove="@(e => Log("OnRemove", "events-left", e))"
                                     OnAdd="@(e => Log("OnAdd", "events-left", e))"
                                     OnSort="@(e => Log("OnSort", "events-left", e))"
                                     OnEnd="@(e => Log("OnEnd", "events-left", e))">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">events-right</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="eventsRight"
                                     TItem="string"
                                     Group="events"
                                     Id="events-right"
                                     OnStart="@(e => Log("OnStart", "events-right", e))"
                                     OnChange="@(e => Log("OnChange", "events-right", e))"
                                     OnUpdate="@(e => Log("OnUpdate", "events-right", e))"
                                     OnRemove="@(e => Log("OnRemove", "events-right", e))"
                                     OnAdd="@(e => Log("OnAdd", "events-right", e))"
                                     OnSort="@(e => Log("OnSort", "events-right", e))"
                                     OnEnd="@(e => Log("OnEnd", "events-right", e))">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="log">
                    @if (eventLog.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <div>No events yet.</div>
                    }
                    @foreach (var line in eventLog)
                    {
                        <div>@line</div>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Keyboard & Strings" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example20CsharpCode" Id="example20">
            <div>
                With <b>Keyboard</b> on (the default) a list is one tab stop: the arrow keys move between its items, Space or
                Enter picks the focused one up, the arrows move it - into the next list of the group too - Home and End to the
                ends, Space or Enter drops it and Escape puts it back. Every step is announced, naming the item by
                <b>ItemLabel</b> and the list by its <b>AriaLabel</b>. <b>Strings</b> rewords or translates what is announced.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Tab enters the list at the item focused last; during a keyboard drag it drops the item where it is, and moves on.</li>
                <li>The arrow keys move the focus to the item in that direction; during a drag they move the item to the next place that way and, past the end of its list, into the nearest list of the group in that direction (here, the right arrow from the Backlog carries an item into the Sprint).</li>
                <li>Home and End focus the first and the last item; during a drag they move the item to the start or the end of its list.</li>
                <li>Space or Enter picks the focused item up, and drops it. Escape puts it back where it was.</li>
                <li>Shift+Space selects or deselects the focused item of a MultiDrag list.</li>
                <li>
                    The arrows follow the layout - up and down a vertical list, along a row, right to left in a right-to-left one,
                    row to row in a grid, from column to column of a board, into a nested list - because the next item, or list,
                    is looked for in the direction of the key.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A keyboard drag moves the item itself, into another list too, and the focus follows it. Every rule applies as for
                    a pointer: a refused position, a full list or a list of another group is announced as refused, ConfirmDrop is
                    asked on the drop, and in Swap mode the arrows move the highlight.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Keys pressed inside an item - on a button, in an input - are left to it: only an item itself, or its drag handle,
                    answers them. Keyboard="false" turns it all off, and a Disabled list is not focusable.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A list is a list of listitems, each described as a "sortable item" (RoleDescription) and pointing to the
                    keyboard instructions (Instructions, through aria-describedby), and named by AriaLabel. Each step is announced
                    through a live region with the item's name, its position and the list it is in; a pointer drag is announced
                    when it starts and when it ends. A drag is announced in the Strings of the list it started in. The texts are
                    functions of a BitSortableAnnouncement, and can be set for every list at once through a BitSortableParams.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The focus outline of an item, and the dashed one of an item picked up, are drawn in forced colors (Windows high
                    contrast) too.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitToggle Label="Keyboard" @bind-Value="keyboard" />
                    <BitToggle Label="Deutsch" @bind-Value="german" />
                </div>
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Backlog</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="ticketBacklog"
                                     Group="tickets"
                                     AriaLabel="Backlog"
                                     ItemLabel="@(ticket => ticket.Title)"
                                     Keyboard="keyboard"
                                     Strings="@(german ? germanStrings : null)">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="ticket">
                                <div class="item">#@ticket.Id @ticket.Title</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Sprint</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="ticketSprint"
                                     Group="tickets"
                                     AriaLabel="Sprint"
                                     ItemLabel="@(ticket => ticket.Title)"
                                     Keyboard="keyboard"
                                     Strings="@(german ? germanStrings : null)">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="ticket">
                                <div class="item">#@ticket.Id @ticket.Title</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Touch & pen" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example21CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example21CodeFiles" Id="example21">
            <div>
                A finger drags an item after resting on it for <b>TouchDelay</b> milliseconds, so a swipe over the list still
                scrolls the page; <b>Delay</b> is the same wait for the mouse and the pen. A pointer that moves more than
                <b>DelayTolerance</b> pixels first is a scroll or a click instead. With <b>ForceFallback</b> the mouse drags the way
                a finger does, with a preview of the item that follows it, instead of the browser's own drag image.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    A mouse drags with the browser's own HTML5 drag and drop, as SortableJS does on the desktop. A finger or a pen
                    drags with pointer events, which work the same in every browser and web view, whether or not it supports HTML5
                    drag and drop on touch: the item stays in its list, dimmed, while a copy of it follows the pointer. Both feed
                    the same hit test and raise the same events, with Input saying which it was. ForceFallback is also for a web
                    view whose native drag and drop does not work.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Once a finger drags an item the page does not scroll under it; near an edge, the drag scrolls the list, a panel
                    around it or the page (see Large list).
                </li>
                <li>
                    Escape, a system gesture that takes the pointer over, or leaving the page cancels a pointer drag and puts
                    everything back. The click a browser sends after a press is swallowed after a drag, so dropping an item does
                    not also click it, and a press on a control inside an item - a button, a link, an input - is left to the control.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The preview is a copy of the item placed above the page, in the Drag part: --bit-Sortable-drag-opacity and
                    --bit-Sortable-drag-shadow restyle it, set on :root since the preview is outside the list.
                </li>
                <li>
                    In the second list a finger on the grip drags at once: the handle has touch-action: none, so the browser does
                    not scroll from it, and TouchDelay is 0. The rest of the row still scrolls the page.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitSlider Label="TouchDelay (ms)" Min="0" Max="1000" Step="50" @bind-Value="touchDelay" />
                    <BitSlider Label="Delay (ms)" Min="0" Max="1000" Step="50" @bind-Value="delay" />
                    <BitSlider Label="DelayTolerance (px)" Min="0" Max="40" @bind-Value="delayTolerance" />
                    <BitToggle Label="ForceFallback" @bind-Value="forceFallback" />
                </div>
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Long press</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="groceries"
                                     TouchDelay="(int)touchDelay"
                                     Delay="(int)delay"
                                     DelayTolerance="(int)delayTolerance"
                                     ForceFallback="forceFallback">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Handle, at once</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="chores" Handle=".handle" TouchDelay="0">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item row">
                                    <BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.GripperDotsVertical" Class="handle" />
                                    <span>@item</span>
                                </div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Large list" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example22CsharpCode" Id="example22">
            <div>
                Three hundred rows in a scrolling panel. Drag a row toward the panel's edge and it scrolls: the browser scrolls for
                the mouse's own drag, the list for a finger, a pen or a ForceFallback mouse - when <b>Scroll</b> is on, within
                <b>ScrollSensitivity</b> pixels of the edge and at up to <b>ScrollSpeed</b> pixels a frame, faster the closer the
                pointer is to the edge. <b>IsolateItems</b> renders each row's template in a component of its own, which a step of
                the drag does not render again.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The pointer is followed and measured in the browser: a step that does not change the drop position never reaches
                    .NET, and one that does is a single call - while it is on its way the browser keeps the latest position for when
                    it returns, so no position is lost and none queues up. That keeps a drag smooth on Blazor Server too.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A new position changes the bound list in place and renders the list once, and the browser slides the rows when
                    that render lands. A render of the list renders all its items' templates; with IsolateItems only the wrappers
                    move. Leave it off when a template shows something that other items' moves change, such as the item's position
                    number: that would only catch up when the drag ends.
                </li>
                <li>
                    From the keyboard, pick Row 1 up and hold the down arrow: the row walks down, and the panel follows it into view.
                </li>
                <li>
                    In the browser's console, BitBlazorUI.Sortable.diagnostics() after a drag shows the positions sent to .NET - one
                    per row crossed, not one per pointer move - and the average and slowest round trip;
                    BitBlazorUI.Sortable.resetDiagnostics() starts over.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A list renders every item it is given: Blazor's Virtualize is not supported, since rows that are not rendered
                    could be neither measured nor dropped beside. Page a list that is too long to render, or let a filter narrow it.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitToggle Label="IsolateItems" @bind-Value="isolateItems" />
                    <BitToggle Label="ForceFallback" @bind-Value="largeForceFallback" />
                    <BitToggle Label="Scroll" @bind-Value="scroll" />
                    <BitSlider Label="ScrollSensitivity (px)" Min="10" Max="120" Step="5" @bind-Value="scrollSensitivity" />
                    <BitSlider Label="ScrollSpeed (px)" Min="2" Max="60" Step="2" @bind-Value="scrollSpeed" />
                </div>
                <div class="panel scroller">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="rows"
                                 IsolateItems="isolateItems"
                                 ForceFallback="largeForceFallback"
                                 Scroll="scroll"
                                 ScrollSensitivity="(int)scrollSensitivity"
                                 ScrollSpeed="(int)scrollSpeed">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="row">
                            <div class="item row">
                                <span class="grow">@row.Name</span>
                                <span class="note">@row.Note</span>
                            </div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="ItemKey" RazorCode="@example23RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example23CsharpCode" Id="example23">
            <div>
                <b>ItemKey</b> says what identifies an item - its id - for rows that a server sends again as new objects. Select two
                rows and load them again: with ItemKey they keep their elements, their focus and their selection, and a drag moves
                the object the list holds now; without it, the new objects are new items to the list, and the selection is gone.
                The key must be unique within the list and stay the same while the row is in it. It is also how the list spots a
                duplicate: a row whose key is already in a list is never dropped into it, so a Clone factory gives a copy a key of
                its own. A lambda written inline is fine: the list tells a new ItemKey by its code, not by the delegate a render
                creates.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="controls">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="ReloadRows">Load the rows again</BitButton>
                    <BitToggle Label="ItemKey" @bind-Value="keyById" />
                </div>
                <div class="panel">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="taskRows" ItemKey="@(keyById ? byId : null)" MultiDrag>
                        <ItemTemplate Context="row">
                            <div class="item">@row.Title</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <div class="order">Loaded @loads time(s)</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example24RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example24CsharpCode" Id="example24">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitSortableParams</b> sets the defaults of every sortable list inside it - put one around
                the app's router for app-wide defaults; a parameter of the list itself still wins (the second list has an EmptyText
                of its own). It carries Animation, Classes, Delay, DelayTolerance, EmptyText, ForceFallback, Keyboard,
                RevertOnSpill, Scroll, ScrollSensitivity, ScrollSpeed, Strings (the announcements in the app's language), Styles,
                SwapThreshold and TouchDelay (a longer long press), besides the parameters every component takes.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <BitParams Parameters="sortableParams">
                    <div class="panels">
                        <div class="panel">
                            <BitSortable @bind-Items="paramsFirst" Group="params">
                                <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                    <div class="item">@item</div>
                                </ItemTemplate>
                            </BitSortable>
                        </div>
                        <div class="panel">
                            <BitSortable @bind-Items="paramsSecond" Group="params" EmptyText="My own empty text.">
                                <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                    <div class="item">@item</div>
                                </ItemTemplate>
                            </BitSortable>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </BitParams>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example25RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example25CsharpCode" Id="example25">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root of the list, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> its parts - the items,
                the drag states and the empty state - and <b>ItemStyle</b> gives each item an inline style of its own, the way
                ItemClass gives it a class. The drag states read the public <b>--bit-Sortable-*</b> variables, which inherit: set
                them on :root, on an ancestor, or on the Style of a list. The last two lists set the ghost, the picked-up item and
                outlines for the lists that take the drag.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Root: the list (bit-srt), with bit-dis while Disabled and bit-srt-hnd with a Handle.</li>
                <li>Item: every item wrapper, and the drop placeholder.</li>
                <li>Ghost: the item being dragged with a pointer, in its list, and the drop placeholder in another list.</li>
                <li>Chosen: the item picked up - also from the keyboard, which wears only this one.</li>
                <li>Drag: the preview that follows a finger, a pen or a ForceFallback mouse, placed above the page.</li>
                <li>SwapTarget, Selected and Filtered: the swap target, the items of a multi-drag selection, and the items a Filter locks.</li>
                <li>Empty: what an empty list shows.</li>
                <li>
                    While a drag runs the script also sets attributes to style, at no render cost: data-bit-srt-accepts on every
                    list that takes the drag, data-bit-srt-over on the one under the pointer, data-bit-srt-refused on that one when
                    it refused the position, and data-bit-srt-dragging on the html element, set to native, pointer or keyboard.
                </li>
                <li>The stylesheet draws only these states, and leaves the look of the list and its items to the page.</li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div class="panels">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Style & Class</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="styledItems"
                                     Style="border:1px dashed var(--bit-clr-brd-pri);border-radius:0.25rem;padding:0 0.25rem"
                                     Class="custom-class">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">Styles & Classes</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="classedItems"
                                     Styles="@(new() { Chosen = "background-color:var(--bit-clr-sec-light)" })"
                                     Classes="@(new() { Item = "custom-item", Ghost = "custom-ghost" })">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <div class="title">ItemStyle</div>
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="itemStyled" ItemStyle="@(item => item == "Important" ? "font-weight:bold" : null)">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="panels" style="@cssVariablesStyle">
                    <div class="panel">
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="fruits" Group="variables">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                    <div class="panel">
                        <BitSortable @bind-Items="basket" Group="variables">
                            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                                <div class="item">@item</div>
                            </ItemTemplate>
                        </BitSortable>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example26RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example26CsharpCode" Id="example26">
            <div>
                Use BitSortable in right-to-left (RTL). The list is measured in its own order, so dragging an item toward the left
                moves it forward - drag the first step to the far left and it becomes the last - and from the keyboard the left
                arrow moves it to the next place.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                <div dir="rtl" class="panel strip">
                    <BitSortable @bind-Items="rtlSteps" Dir="BitDir.Rtl">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="step">
                            <div class="chip">@step</div>
                        </ItemTemplate>
                    </BitSortable>
                </div>
                <div dir="rtl" class="order">ترتیب: @string.Join("، ", rtlSteps)</div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>

@code {
    // A list inside every item: each level shares the group, so an item moves from branch to branch with its children.
    private RenderFragment NestedList(IList<TreeNode> nodes) =>
        @<BitSortable TItem="TreeNode" Items="nodes" ItemsChanged="StateHasChanged" Group="tree" SwapThreshold="0.65" EmptyText="">
            <ItemTemplate Context="node">
                <div class="item">@node.Name</div>
                <div class="children">
                    @NestedList(node.Children)
                </div>
            </ItemTemplate>
        </BitSortable>;
}
